zefciu <zefirek@Speacock.Pau.Apoznan.Mpl> writes:

> What are the best classes for the particular conducts?  I put my guesses
> here, but wait for your opinions:

> pacifist - no opinion.  For me it's still unbelieveable idea.  But maybe
> tourist with his charm monster bonus or healer with level draining weapon?

For roleplaying, healers might be best suited to the idea.
But for gameplay, I find it very hard with any class.

> illiterate = never tried, but I think it goes with hack'n'slash-ness of
> the class.  Worst - except spellcasters I can't imagine tourist without
> E-word.

Yep, wizards probably find illiteracy harder than others.
For roleplaying, probably barbarians and cavepeople.
For gameplay, this is another conduct hard for any class.

> genoless - wizard and all neutral characters (magicbane against liches)

I have ascended several times genoless, most of them valkyries (but then
again, most of my ascensions overall are valkyries).
I think generally the fighter classes have least need for genociding,
and monks have most use for it, especially if they want to keep the
weaponless conduct.

> polyselfless, polypileless, wishless - don't know.  My first ascension

I generally tend to keep these conducts (and genoless) in every game as
long as I can, and I don't think the character class makes much difference
with these, except that rogues are hardest for polyselfless.
Polyselfless is mostly about avoiding polymorph traps. Wizards start with
magic resistance, so it is easiest for them.
Polypileless and wishless depend on which essential item(s) you don't find
in the dungeon, not on which class you play.

